	('d3b1cad5-a877-4b34-8d9e-8ebe43d90de0', 'c4d83a6e-28fb-4ac3-9146-4892895595a4', NULL, 'Migrate to BleacherTypes', '<p>make sure you add the bleacher_type_uuid into the Bleachers table and then deprecate some columns in the Bleachers table.</p>', 'to_do', 0, false, NULL, '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '2026-06-01 13:18:31.889+00', NULL, NULL),
	('6cfb053f-93f8-4cc8-bfd0-f2a18c3ac259', 'c4d83a6e-28fb-4ac3-9146-4892895595a4', NULL, 'Sales Scorecard - Add Dropdown to week/quarter/year to select which week/quarter/year', '<p></p>', 'to_do', 0, true, '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '2026-06-02 13:38:06.316+00', NULL, NULL),
	('38ab9be2-fe90-46cf-9b0b-d5a31f4b7632', 'c4d83a6e-28fb-4ac3-9146-4892895595a4', NULL, 'Figure out how to get tax codes', '<p></p><p>I’ll verify against Intuit’s current docs rather than guessing, because QuickBooks tax behavior depends on region and whether the company uses Automated Sales Tax.</p><p>Thought for 32s</p><p>Yes, QuickBooks does offer ways to calculate sales tax, but there are two different things people often mix up.</p><p>The answer depends on what you want:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>A) “I am creating a QuickBooks invoice and want QuickBooks to calculate the tax.”
B) “I want to type an address in my own app and get a tax estimate before creating the invoice.”</code></pre><p>For <strong>A</strong>, QuickBooks Online’s normal Invoice API can calculate tax when you create/update an invoice. For US QuickBooks companies using Automated Sales Tax, QuickBooks determines tax from the company/source address and the transaction shipping/destination address. It returns the calculated amount in <code>TxnTaxDetail.TotalTax</code>. </p><p>For <strong>B</strong>, QuickBooks has a newer <strong>Sales Tax API</strong> that can calculate tax outside of a QuickBooks invoice. It uses GraphQL and supports a request with transaction date, customer, ship-from address, ship-to address, shipping fee, and line items. It returns tax amounts and tax breakdowns.  However, this API is only available to Silver, Gold, and Platinum Intuit partners, not normal Builder-tier apps. It also requires the restricted OAuth scope <code>indirect-tax.tax-calculation.quickbooks</code>. </p><p>So the practical answer is:</p><p><strong>If you want the user to type an address in your Next.js app and instantly preview tax before creating an invoice, you probably need the QuickBooks Sales Tax API — but you may not have access unless you are on a paid Intuit partner tier.</strong></p><p>For a normal QuickBooks Online integration, the more realistic flow is:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>1. User creates/edits invoice/event in your app.
2. You send the invoice to QuickBooks with taxable line items.
3. QuickBooks calculates tax.
4. You read back `TxnTaxDetail.TotalTax`.
5. You store that tax amount on your invoice/event record.</code></pre><p>For US companies using QuickBooks Automated Sales Tax, taxable lines should use:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>{
  "SalesItemLineDetail": {
    "TaxCodeRef": {
      "value": "TAX"
    }
  }
}</code></pre><p>QuickBooks says taxable sales item lines use <code>TaxCodeRef.value = TAX</code>, and then tax is calculated and returned in <code>TxnTaxDetail.TotalTax</code>. </p><p>For non-US companies, including likely Canadian QuickBooks files, the REST invoice model is different. You generally assign an actual <code>TaxCodeRef</code> to each taxable invoice line, and QuickBooks calculates tax from that tax code, returning the result in <code>TxnTaxDetail.TotalTax</code>.  That means QuickBooks may not simply take an address and decide “Ontario HST” for you through the normal Invoice REST API. Your app may need to map the venue/customer address to the correct tax code, such as Ontario HST, Alberta GST, etc., then pass that tax code on each line.</p><p>For your app, I would design it like this:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>EventLineItems
- id
- event_uuid
- description
- quantity
- unit_price_cents
- subtotal_cents
- taxable
- quickbooks_item_id
- quickbooks_tax_code_id
- tax_cents
- total_cents

Events
- id
- subtotal_cents
- tax_cents
- total_cents
- quickbooks_invoice_id
- tax_calculation_source -- quickbooks, manual, cached, etc.
- tax_calculated_at</code></pre><p>Then use this flow:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>When editing the invoice:
- Calculate subtotal yourself.
- Determine taxable line items.
- Determine destination province/state from the venue/customer address.

If you have access to QuickBooks Sales Tax API:
- Call it from a Next.js server route.
- Store returned tax amount and breakdown.

If you do not have Sales Tax API access:
- For Canada/non-US: map address → QuickBooks TaxCodeRef.
- For US AST-enabled companies: create/update a draft invoice in QBO and let QBO calculate.
- Read back TxnTaxDetail.TotalTax.</code></pre><p>Important: do this from a <strong>server-side Next.js route</strong>, not from the browser. Your access token, refresh token, client secret, and QuickBooks realm ID should not be exposed to the frontend.</p><p>A simplified server-side shape would look like this:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>// app/api/quickbooks/tax-preview/route.ts

export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const body = await req.json();

  const {
    realmId,
    customerId,
    shipToAddress,
    lineItems,
  } = body;

  // 1. Get QuickBooks OAuth access token from your DB/session.
  const accessToken = await getQuickBooksAccessToken();

  // 2. Either call QuickBooks Sales Tax API if you have access,
  // or create/update a QBO invoice and read back TxnTaxDetail.TotalTax.

  // 3. Return normalized tax info to your frontend.
  return Response.json({
    subtotalCents: 660000,
    taxCents: 85800,
    totalCents: 745800,
    source: "quickbooks",
  });
}</code></pre><p>If you use the premium GraphQL Sales Tax API, the request is conceptually like this:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>mutation CalculateTax {
  indirectTaxCalculateSaleTransactionTax(
    input: {
      transactionDate: "2026-05-29"
      subject: {
        qbCustomerId: "1"
      }
      shipping: {
        shipFromAddress: {
          freeFormAddressLine: "Your company address"
        }
        shipToAddress: {
          freeFormAddressLine: "Customer/event address"
        }
      }
      lineItems: [
        {
          numberOfUnits: 3
          pricePerUnitExcludingTaxes: {
            value: 2200
          }
          productVariantTaxability: {
            productVariantId: 1
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ) {
    taxCalculation {
      taxTotals {
        totalTaxAmountExcludingShipping {
          value
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre><p>That is based on Intuit’s documented Sales Tax API use case, which shows <code>indirectTaxCalculateSaleTransactionTax</code> accepting ship-from/ship-to addresses and line items, then returning tax amounts and tax details. </p><p>For your bleacher rental invoicing feature, I would not make QuickBooks the only source of truth while the user is editing. I would store your own event line items, subtotal, tax estimate, and total. Then, when creating the actual QuickBooks invoice, send the line items to QuickBooks and store the final QuickBooks invoice ID plus the returned tax amount.</p><p>My recommendation:</p><pre><code></code></pre><pre><code>Best option if you have Sales Tax API access:
Use QuickBooks Sales Tax API for tax preview, then create the QBO invoice.

Most realistic option for normal QBO API access:
Use your own address → tax-code mapping in your app, then let QBO calculate final tax when the invoice is created.

Avoid:
Trying to call the normal Invoice API as a pure “tax lookup by address” service. That is not really what the REST Invoice API is designed for.</code></pre><p></p>', 'completed', 0, false, '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '2026-05-28 19:53:20.722+00', '2026-06-01 17:47:11.798103+00', NULL),
	('cd44f9f9-7067-4530-9552-5ae001ed750f', NULL, NULL, 'Dashboard and KPI''s', '<p>I''d like to create a dashboard with metrics that are public for all to see. But i''m woundering if we need a private list that shows only the login users events in a list sorted by date, so they can easily switch the event to confirmed, rather then going in the dashboard list. Anyways, forward looking metrics are. # of Quotes sent, Value of quotes sent # of signed quotes, Value of signed quotes, Evergreen metrics. (past looking) Main metric = ROI on assets under managerment. (total asset value under manager / Gross profit) Sub KPI''s to get this number, Total value of events (using event starting date for entire event value) Total costs of delivery, =. all work trackers totals. Gross margin percentage = total event value divided by total costs of delvery(work trackers) Gross profit = Total event value - total cost of delvery Total bleacher value, = total value of bleachers under management, (winter is dec 1 to juine 1, summer is june 1 to dec 1,). we will require to put value of each asset in the suystem for this.</p>', 'completed', 0, true, NULL, '6b080032-c889-4ce5-86e0-5a564a4d97f7', '2025-11-13 14:09:10.685741+00', '2026-06-02 13:38:21.818685+00', NULL),
	('57dcee49-0109-4944-941f-db1224ab594f', 'c4d83a6e-28fb-4ac3-9146-4892895595a4', '99776dbb-8ab2-4d98-bc73-d808d106bc5e', 'Setup Stripe Service', '<p>Going to create a Stripe service where Stripe just uses the payment intent without using products or prices or anything like that inside of Stripe. Stripe will just be the means for processing payments, not storing product or payment or pricing information. </p>', 'completed', 0, false, '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '2026-05-28 19:53:01.272+00', '2026-06-01 18:28:16.674538+00', NULL),
	('b100c76f-2720-47ac-9daf-d5ea7837ddc9', 'c4d83a6e-28fb-4ac3-9146-4892895595a4', '99776dbb-8ab2-4d98-bc73-d808d106bc5e', 'Use tax-percent endpoint', '<p>I created a new endpoint you can test on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://localhost:3000/dev-tools/qbo-get-sales-tax">http://localhost:3000/dev-tools/qbo-get-sales-tax</a></p><p>use this in the quote!<br><br>explanation: <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.loom.com/share/e45e4c62a9ca46a3b96aeeba01e3c739">https://www.loom.com/share/e45e4c62a9ca46a3b96aeeba01e3c739</a></p>', 'completed', 0, false, '43f82f56-f6e2-4400-aeb2-7c5195c557ad', '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '2026-06-01 15:57:51.067+00', '2026-06-01 21:22:55.111194+00', NULL),
	('a5c961f6-56d9-44f9-b3eb-07783fd5804d', 'c4d83a6e-28fb-4ac3-9146-4892895595a4', '99776dbb-8ab2-4d98-bc73-d808d106bc5e', 'Create Quotes Page', '<p>Try to copy the general layout idea from Goodshuffle - like their top navigation stuff but you could maybe use something more consistent with our style like src/app/assets/bleachers/_lib/components/TabNavigation.tsx</p>', 'in_progress', 0, false, '43f82f56-f6e2-4400-aeb2-7c5195c557ad', '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '2026-05-28 19:48:53.228+00', NULL, NULL),
	('fb470efa-f940-4b97-afa9-75fc61bab290', 'c4d83a6e-28fb-4ac3-9146-4892895595a4', NULL, 'Dashboard Address Tooltip - Only change address on confirmed bookings', '<p>quoted events should not affect address tooltip.</p>', 'to_do', 0, true, '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '9bddb34c-d84e-4d11-852a-27f16de08379', '2026-06-02 13:27:39.092+00', NULL, NULL),
